"The typical structure of Gallai colorings and their extremal graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07747)\nciteturn7academia11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.9 years ago; Title: The typical structure of Gallai colorings and their extremal graphs ... We prove that the complete graph $K_n$ admits the largest number of Gallai $3$-colorings among all $n$-vertex graphs when $n$ is sufficiently large, while for $r\\geq 4$, it is the complete bipartite graph $K_{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor, \\lceil n/2 \\rceil}$.\nTitle: The typical structure of Gallai colorings and their extremal graphs\nAuthors: József Balogh, Lina Li\nDate: Wed Dec 19 04:14:03 2018\n\nAn edge coloring of a graph $G$ is a Gallai coloring if it contains no rainbow triangle. We show that the number of Gallai $r$-colorings of $K_n$ is $\\left(\\binom{r}{2}+o(1)\\right)2^{\\binom{n}{2}}$. This result indicates that almost all Gallai $r$-colorings of $K_n$ use only 2 colors. We also study the extremal behavior of Gallai $r$-colorings among all $n$-vertex graphs. We prove that the complete graph $K_n$ admits the largest number of Gallai $3$-colorings among all $n$-vertex graphs when $n$ is sufficiently large, while for $r\\geq 4$, it is the complete bipartite graph $K_{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor, \\lceil n/2 \\rceil}$. Our main approach is based on the hypergraph container method, developed independently by Balogh, Morris, and Samotij as well as by Saxton and Thomason, together with some stability results for containers.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtremal problems and results related to Gallai-colorings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.04879)\nciteturn7academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Title: Extremal problems and results related to Gallai-colorings ... A Gallai-coloring (Gallai-$k$-coloring) is an edge-coloring (with colors from $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, k\\}$) of a complete graph without rainbow triangles.\nTitle: Extremal problems and results related to Gallai-colorings\nAuthors: Xihe Li, Hajo Broersma, Ligong Wang\nDate: Mon Jun  8 19:00:23 2020\n\nA Gallai-coloring (Gallai-$k$-coloring) is an edge-coloring (with colors from $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, k\\}$) of a complete graph without rainbow triangles. Given a graph $H$ and a positive integer $k$, the $k$-colored Gallai-Ramsey number $GR_k(H)$ is the minimum integer $n$ such that every Gallai-$k$-coloring of the complete graph $K_n$ contains a monochromatic copy of $H$. In this paper, we consider two extremal problems related to Gallai-$k$-colorings. First, we determine upper and lower bounds for the maximum number of edges that are not contained in any rainbow triangle or monochromatic triangle in a $k$-edge-coloring of $K_n$. Second, for $n\\geq GR_k(K_3)$, we determine upper and lower bounds for the minimum number of monochromatic triangles in a Gallai-$k$-coloring of $K_{n}$, yielding the exact value for $k=3$. Furthermore, we determine the Gallai-Ramsey number $GR_k(K_4+e)$ for the graph on five vertices consisting of a $K_4$ with a pendant edge.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - TauCetiProject/TauCetiRoadmap: Human-controlled roadmaps for Tau Ceti, an AIs-welcome Lean library downstream of Mathlib. · GitHub (https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCetiRoadmap)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The human-controlled roadmaps for Tau Ceti, an AIs-welcome Lean 4 library downstream of Mathlib. ...   * Modular curves, following Katz–Mazur\n  * Modular curves, following Katz–Mazur\n  * Modular forms — Hecke theory, newforms, and L-functions\n  * Morse and Floer homology with DG local coefficients\n  * Number fields, ramification, Frobenius, and the LMFDB invariants\n  * One-parameter semigroups, completely monotone functions, and BCR Bochner\n  * Operator theory\n  * Optimal transport and Wasserstein geometry\n  * Orthogonal and spin groups\n  * P-adic measures, completed group algebras, and characteristic ideals\n  * Partial differential equations\n  * Peripheral actions on free pro-`p` groups\n  * Pivotal and spherical categories\n  * Profinite and pro-`p` groups\n  * Profinite integers, profinite powers, and continuous automorphisms\n  * Quadratic forms and cohomological invariants\n  * Real algebraic geometry: sign determination and cylindrical decomposition\n  * Reductive algebraic groups\n  * Representation theory (semisimple algebras, character tables, Lie and classical groups, Schur-Weyl, Peter-Weyl)\n  * Scheme, stack, cohomology and intersection foundations\n  * Stable reduction of curves and stable maps\n  * Stable, periodic, and curved homological algebra\n  * Standard probability distributions and their elementary theory\n  * The Chebotarev density theorem\n  * The Jacobian challenge\n  * Zigzag, preprojective, and Ginzburg algebras\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy seaweed in naming the tool? · seaweedfs seaweedfs · Discussion #6095 · GitHub (https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/discussions/6095)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Why seaweed in naming the tool?\n\n# Why seaweed in naming the tool? #6095\n\nAnswered by chrislusf\n\nCKHH asked this question in Q&A\n\nWhy seaweed in naming the tool? #6095\n\nOct 6, 2024 · 3 comments · 2 replies\n\nDiscussion options\n\n## \n\nCKHH Oct 6, 2024\n\nWhy is seaweed used in naming the tool ”SeaweeFS”? Just curious what’s the story behind it. Thanks.\n\n\nAnswered by chrislusf Oct 7, 2024\n\nOriginally to follow Haystack the name theme, it was named as wild grass (weed). As a Chinese poem says, \"Wild fire can not kill them. The spring breeze blows and gives birth again.\"\n\nLater, Japanese users said weed is just a very bad word. (Not very sure).\n\nSo the name was changed to Seaweed, which is \"healthier\". But the binary name is still \"weed\".\n\nView full answer\n\nCKHH Oct 6, 2024\n\nbtw it is an awesome tool 👍🏼\n\nYou must be logged in to vote\n\nAll reactions\n\n0 replies\n\nchrislusf Oct 7, 2024\n\nOriginally to follow Haystack the name theme, it was named as wild grass (weed). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - microbetrainer/Seaweed: Code and data from seaweed paper · GitHub (https://github.com/microbetrainer/Seaweed)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Seaweed_Analysis.Rmd  | Seaweed_Analysis.Rmd  |    |\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \nSeaweed_Analysis.Rmd  | Seaweed_Analysis.Rmd  |    |  \nmeta.csv  | meta.csv  |    |  \nmetadata.txt  | metadata.txt  |    |  \nseqtab_final.rds  | seqtab_final.rds  |    |  \ntax_RDP.rds  | tax_RDP.rds  |    |  \ntax_final.rds  | tax_final.rds  |    |  \n\n\n  *   * README\n\nMore items\n\n# Seaweed\n\nCode and data from seaweed paper\n\n## About\n\nCode and data from seaweed paper\n\n\nReadme\n\nActivity\n\n### Stars\n\n0 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks\n\n## Releases\n\n## Packages\n\n## Contributors\n\nYou can’t perform that action at this time.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngraph/CHANGELOG.md at main · statelyai/graph · GitHub (https://github.com/statelyai/graph/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;   * #28 `0498d52` Thanks @davidkpiano! - Analytical coverage tail: cores, Katz, bipartite matching, min-cut, seeded label propagation, and graph generators.\nCompound nodes map to cytoscape parents.\n\nThe package smoke test exercises all nine layout entry points against the packed tarball.\n\n  * #28 `0498d52` Thanks @davidkpiano! - Analytical coverage tail: cores, Katz, bipartite matching, min-cut, seeded label propagation, and graph generators.\n\n    * k-core — `getCoreNumbers(graph)` (Batagelj–Zaveršnik, O(m)) and `getKCore(graph